{"t":1793698286,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"636c8e9f-d41b-4de2-b704-78e52729b450","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-03T07:35:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 030735\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n235 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nMinor tweaks to temperatures and cloud cover today through Wednesday \nas benign weather continues. Update timing and PoPs late week as a \nseries of upper level troughs pass through. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Above normal temperatures and dry conditions through\n  Wednesday with increasing rain chances Thursday into early\n  next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and dry conditions through \nWednesday with increasing rain chances Thursday into early next\nweek.\n\nIt's another dry day ahead with passing high level clouds from time \nto time as upper level ridging remains entrenched over the region. \nSurface high pressure will continue to meander offshore before \nshifting further out into the western Atlantic Wednesday. As a \nresult, expect mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies for today and \nWednesday with a steady supply of southerly return flow. \n\nExpect pockets of patchy fog this morning with temperatures starting \nout in the 40s. Highs will quickly warm into the 50s and 60s this \nmorning with low to mid 70s this afternoon. Lows tonight will fall \nback into the upper 40s and low 50s with highs back in the low to \nmid 70s Wednesday. A few locations could punch into the upper 70s in \nthe lee of Alleghenies and south/east of Washington DC Wednesday \nafternoon given increased southerly flow. \n\nOverall Wednesday will be our day of transition given the buckling \nridge/departing high pressure offshore and incumbent upper level \ntrough approaching from the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes region. This \nwill lead to increasing clouds Wednesday afternoon with rain chances \nlikely arriving from the west as early as Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night. Current 18z/00z guidance suggests a weaker system \nfor Thursday leading to lower rain amounts. This is largely due in \npart to the trough passing north and west of the region from the \nnorthern Ohio River Valley/eastern Great Lakes keeping the bulk of \nthe forcing north of the region. The resultant surface front is set \nto cross sometime Thursday morning into Thursday afternoon leading \nto scattered showers and a few t-storms. Rainfall amounts Thursday \nwill range between a tenth to a quarter of an inch with locally \nhigher totals west of the Alleghenies where better forcing resides. \nDespite increased cloud cover expect highs back into the low to mid \n70s (60s mountains) Thursday afternoon. Storm total rain through \nFriday night will generally be between a quarter to a half an inch. \n\nA secondary upper level trough and associated cold frontal boundary \nwill cross the region Friday into Saturday. Once again this system \nwill be fairly progressive per the past few model cycles. This \nsystem looks to skip across the Alleghenies and Blue Ridge before \nredeveloping east of I-95. This is due largely in part to the upper \nlevel trough cutting off over the Great Lakes Friday evening into \nSaturday morning. At the surface, this will yield a dry slot ahead \nof the cold frontal boundary leading to higher rain chances over the \nmountains and in areas along/east of I-95. With that said, NBM PoPs \ncontinue to remain too high based upon this set up versus the \ndeterministic/ensemble guidance. Weak high pressure will slide in \nfrom the southwest Saturday into Sunday cutting down rain chances \nover the weekend ahead. \n\nThe high will be short-lived with another strong trough set to cross \nthe area late Sunday into Monday. This system appears much larger \nthan the previous two and more potent in delivering an airmass \nchange for next week. Periods of showers are possible late Sunday \nnight through early Tuesday morning although timing, coverage, and \nplacement are yet to be determined. Upslope precip may linger \nTuesday into Wednesday within northwest flow. Highs Monday and \nTuesday will return back into the upper 50s and mid 60s (40s \nmountains). \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions and light south/southeasterly winds at \nless than 15 kts will continue through Wednesday as high pressure \nmeanders over the western Atlantic. Some patchy fog is possible at \nterminals along and east of I-95 this morning and again \ntonight/Wednesday morning given the uptick in moisture advection. \nThis will lead to brief reductions in cigs/vsbys during the 9-13z/4-\n8am time window. VFR conditions continue into Thursday although \nbrief sub-VFR reductions are possible at times with showers and even \na few thunderstorms Thursday morning into Thursday afternoon. South \nto southwesterly winds continue at less than 15 kts. \n\nAdditional sub-VFR reductions are possible at times Friday into \nFriday night as a secondary trough of low pressure/front push \nthrough. VFR conditions will prevail overall although showers during \nthe morning and afternoon/evening hours over the mountains and at \nterminals along/east of I-95 could lead to brief reductions. Winds \nwill shift to the west Friday and eventually northwest Friday night \ninto Saturday. VFR conditions return Saturday into Sunday with \nanother round of sub-VFR conditions late Sunday night into Monday as \na stronger trough pushes through. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through Wednesday morning. Winds will \nremain light out of the south and southeast through Wednesday as \nhigh pressure pushes offshore. By Wednesday evening into Thursday \nmorning, expect the potential for southerly channeling due to the \nhigh offshore and incumbent trough from the northern Ohio River \nValley. Confidence in this occurring is low with a focus over the \nmiddle and open waters. \n\nLow pressure and a strong cold front will work across the waters \nThursday into Friday. Widespread showers and perhaps a few \nthunderstorms are possible Thursday afternoon. Winds will remain \njust below SCA levels both days with westerly gusts under 15 kts. \nWeak high pressure returns Saturday before another strong front \ncrosses late Sunday into Monday. This front will bring a \ngreater likelihood of SCA conditions across the waters heading \ninto early next week.\n\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CPB/EST\nAVIATION...EST\nMARINE...EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793698222,"days":{"2026-11-03":{"hi":73,"lo":52,"pop":2,"short":"Patchy Fog then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":76,"lo":61,"pop":18,"short":"Patchy Fog then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-05":{"hi":76,"lo":61,"pop":71,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-06":{"hi":72,"lo":53,"pop":76,"short":"Light Rain"},"2026-11-07":{"hi":69,"lo":47,"pop":13,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-08":{"hi":64,"lo":45,"pop":26,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-09":{"hi":61,"lo":null,"pop":26,"short":"Chance Light Rain"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793696400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[9.0,8.9,8.7,8.2,6.4,9.4,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.8,9.6,9.5,9.5,9.7,9.8,9.4,7.9,6.4,5.1,6.1,7.7,9.4,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[1,1,1,1,1,3,4,5,6,6,6,6,6,6,5,4,4,4,4,3,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,8,8,7,7,6,5,5,4,4,4,4,4,4],"gst":[5,5,5,5,6,7,8,9,10,9,10,10,11,10,10,9,9,9,9,8,8,7,7,6,6,5,5,5,6,7,7,8,9,11,12,13,13,13,12,12,11,10,1